<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · concept</p><h1 class="enc-title">Witchcraft</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as witch, witches</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Witchcraft is the use of magic by a person called a witch, traditionally to inflict supernatural harm or misfortune on others. Belief in witches is attested from ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and has been found in a great many societies, which shunned, banished, punished or killed alleged witches. In medieval and early modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> the accused were usually women believed to have used maleficium against their own community, and the witch-hunts and witch trials of the early modern period led to tens of thousands of executions. European belief dwindled during and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Age of Enlightenment</a>; modern witch-hunting takes place in parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p></div>
